{"records":[{"id":"rhgb7xcv5c","title":"BUB1 and CENP-U, primed by CDK1, are the main PLK1 kinetochore receptors in mitosis","description":"Reflecting its pleiotropic functions, Polo-like kinase 1 (PLK1) localizes to various sub-cellular structures during mitosis. At kinetochores, PLK1 contributes to microtubule attachments and mitotic checkpoint signaling. Previous studies identified a wealth of potential PLK1 receptors at kinetochores, as well as requirements for various mitotic kinases, including BUB1, Aurora B, and PLK1 itself. Here, we combine ectopic localization, in vitro reconstitution, and kinetochore localization studies to demonstrate that most and likely all of the PLK1 is recruited through BUB1 in the outer kinetochore and centromeric protein U (CENP-U) in the inner kinetochore. BUB1 and CENP-U share a constellation of sequence motifs consisting of a putative PP2A-docking motif and two neighboring PLK1-docking sites, which, contingent on priming phosphorylation by cyclin-dependent kinase 1 and PLK1 itself, bind PLK1 and promote its dimerization. Our results rationalize previous observations and describe a unifying mechanism for recruitment of PLK1 to human kinetochores.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-02T14:27:00.098Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhgb7xcv5c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhgb7xcv5c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Marion","last_name":"Pesenti","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Division","Mitosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hzmt5skpdy","title":"PLK1 blockade enhances the anti-tumor effect of MAPK inhibition in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","description":"Original Western Blot Images from the figures displayed in the manuscript: PLK1 blockade enhances the anti-tumor effect of MAPK inhibition in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-27T12:28:29.25Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hzmt5skpdy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hzmt5skpdy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ben","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jens","last_name":"Siveke","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6jnkjbd8k","title":"Centromere/kinetochore is assembled through CENP-C oligomerization. Hara et al","description":"The kinetochore is an essential protein complex required for accurate chromosome segregation. The constitutive centromere-associated network (CCAN), a subcomplex of the kinetochore, associates with centromeric chromatin and provides a platform for the kinetochore assembly. The CCAN protein CENP-C is thought to be a central hub for the centromere/kinetochore organization. However, the role of CENP-C in CCAN assembly needs to be elucidated. Here, we demonstrate that both the CCAN-binding domain and the C-terminal region that includes the Cupin domain of CENP-C are necessary and sufficient for chicken CENP-C function. Structural and biochemical analyses reveal self-oligomerization of the Cupin domains of chicken and human CENP-C. We find that the CENP-C Cupin domain oligomerization is vital for CENP-C function, centromeric localization of CCAN, and centromeric chromatin organization. These results suggest that CENP-C facilitates the centromere/kinetochore assembly through its oligomerization.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-01T17:52:13.253Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6jnkjbd8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6jnkjbd8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Masatoshi","last_name":"Hara","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Centromere","Chromosome Segregation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w8jtmnszd9","title":"The Proteomic Landscape of Genome-Wide Genetic Perturbations","description":"Functional genomic strategies have become fundamental for annotating gene function and regulatory networks. Here, we combined functional genomics with proteomics by quantifying protein abundances in a genome-scale knock-out library in Saccharomyces cerevisiae, using data-independent acquisition mass spectrometry. We thus created a large, systematic, and quantitative proteomic dataset, with an average of 2,520 proteins quantified across 4,699 yeast gene knock-out strains. The proteome profiles comprise over 100 million peptide quantitations and 9 million protein quantitations. These link deleted genes to proteins and provide a genome-scale resource of molecular phenotypes for 79% of the coding yeast genome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-11-22T06:58:00.663Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w8jtmnszd9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w8jtmnszd9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christoph","last_name":"Messner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Georg","last_name":"Kustatscher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Ralser","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Systems Biology","Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dt2ncczrms","title":"Dismissal of RNA Polymerase II Underlies a Large Ligand-induced Enhancer Decommissioning Program","description":"Nuclear receptors induce both transcriptional activation and repression programs responsible for development, homeostasis, and disease. Here, we report a previously overlooked enhancer decommissioning strategy underlying a large estrogen receptor alpha (ERα)-dependent transcriptional repression program. The unexpected signature for this E2-induced program resides in indirect recruitment of ERα to a large cohort of pioneer factor basally active FOXA1-bound enhancers that lack cognate ERα DNA-binding elements. Surprisingly, these basally active estrogen-repressed (BAER) enhancers are decommissioned by ERα-dependent recruitment of the histone demethylase KDM2A, functioning independently of its demethylase activity. Rather, KDM2A tethers the E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase NEDD4 to ubiquitylate/dismiss Pol II to abrogate eRNA transcription, with consequent target gene downregulation. Thus, our data reveal that Pol II ubiquitylation/dismissal may serve as a potentially broad strategy utilized by indirectly bound nuclear receptors to abrogate large programs of pioneer factor-mediated, eRNA-producing enhancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-10-02T16:28:51.832Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dt2ncczrms","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dt2ncczrms"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuliang","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"tp3j5hm3dj","title":"Causal Connectivity Measured By Single-Cell Perturbations","description":"Data accompanying the article: \"Single-Cell Perturbations Reveal Selective Modulation of Causal Connectivity During Decision-Making\".\nHere: Causal connectivity per target-responder pair, along with other calculated properties of the target and responder neuron. Also, m-files to regenerate figure panels in the main text (Matlab).","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-08T03:58:50.535Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tp3j5hm3dj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tp3j5hm3dj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mark","last_name":"Ioffe","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"5U19NS132720"}],"categories":["Neuroscience","Decision Making"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xjwm5ygymn","title":"Plk1 gene regulatory network involved in genomic instability","description":"Recently, the dynamics behind the events of genomic instability have gained relevance due to their association with specific genes and cellular mechanisms, which are presented as new and powerful therapeutic alternatives for the disease. Previous studies found the gene Plk1 as a candidate for genomic instability; however, the mechanisms by which the gene and its dysregulation could induce genomic instability events weren’t entirely clear. Therefore, to elucidate the mechanisms, gene circuits, and proteins involved in the genomic instability events promoted by Plk1, a novel approach that involved the reconstruction and modeling of the regulatory network of Plk1 was adopted. The network contains nine processes where Plk1 participates that are involved in maintaining the stability of the genome. Subsequently, mathematical modeling of the regulation network was carried out under a mass action law kinetic approximation to generate a model with 1030 reactions and 716 biological species. The simulations allowed the identification of three interaction circuits between proteins that can potentially induce a genomic instability event as a product of Plk1 dysregulation. Additionally, eight proteins previously associated with genomic instability were identified within the circuits and proposed to amplify the genome instability event. As a novelty, the proteins KIF2C and INCENP are postulated as candidates to participate in the genomic instability process. The information contained herein can be used to feed future models, which are built under similar approaches.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-20T21:19:19.459Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xjwm5ygymn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xjwm5ygymn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Suescum H","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Diana Carolina","last_name":"Clavijo Buriticá","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mauricio Alberto","last_name":"Quimbaya Gomez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Edward Fabian","last_name":"Carrillo Borda","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mathematical Modeling","Cancer Systems Biology","Gene Regulation","Biological Network","Genomic Instability"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jwbf2cx23d","title":"SKAP binding to microtubules reduces friction at the kinetochore-microtubule interface and increases attachment stability under force","description":"Data associated to the paper \"The Astrin-SKAP Complex Reduces Friction at the Kinetochore-Microtubule Interface\", Rosas-Salvans et al., Current Biology, DOI: 10.1016/j.cub.2022.04.061. \n\nHere we include the data used for the figures in this paper (files are created and must be opened using GraphPad-Prism or Microsoft Excel). The raw data and Phyton code used for the kinetochore oscillations analysis can be found at the following link: https://github.com/miquelrosassalvans/Rosas-Salvans-2024-kinetochore-oscillations\n\nSummary of the work: The kinetochore links chromosomes to spindle microtubules to drive chromosome segregation at cell division. We recently uncovered that the kinetochore complex Astrin-SKAP, which binds microtubules, reduces rather than increases friction at the mammalian kinetochore-microtubule interface. How it does so is not known. Astrin-SKAP could affect how other kinetochore complexes bind microtubules, reducing their friction along microtubules, or it could itself bind microtubules with similar affinity but lower friction than other attachment factors. Using SKAP mutants unable to bind microtubules, live imaging and laser ablation, we show that SKAP’s microtubule binding is essential for sister kinetochore coordination, force dissipation at the interface and attachment responsiveness to force changes. Further, we show that SKAP’s microtubule binding is essential to prevent chromosome detachment under both spindle forces and microneedle-generated forces. Together, our findings indicate that SKAP’s microtubule binding reduces kinetochore friction and increases attachment responsiveness and stability under force. We propose that having complexes with both high and low sliding friction on microtubules, making a mechanically heterogeneous interface, is key to maintaining robust attachments under force and thus accurate segregation.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-17T16:43:14.531Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jwbf2cx23d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jwbf2cx23d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"miquel","last_name":"rosas-salvans","orcid":"0000-0002-5397-121X"},{"first_name":"caleb","last_name":"rux","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Moumita","last_name":"Das","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sophie","last_name":"Dumont","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Biophysics","Cell Division","Microtubule","Chromosome Segregation","Mitosis","Mitotic Spindle"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"g7z3pw3bfw","title":"Scalable and universal prediction of cellular phenotypes","description":"Input embeddings for Prophet, containing 115k treatments and 1.4k cell lines. Treatments are identified by their SMILES string for compounds or gene target for genetic perturbations. Cell lines are identified by their common name.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-15T21:22:51.33Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/g7z3pw3bfw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/g7z3pw3bfw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Tejada Lapuerta","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuge","last_name":"Ji","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Discovery","Cell Biology","Phenotyping","Biomarker Discovery","Cell-Based Assays"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"srrd35bxcm","title":"Evolution of fatty acid taste in drosophilids","description":"Comparative studies of related but ecologically-distinct species can reveal how thenervous system evolves to drive behaviors that are particularly suited to a given environment. Drosophila melanogaster is a generalist that feeds and oviposits on most overripe fruits. A related species, D. sechellia, is an obligate specialist of Morinda citrifolia (noni) fruit, which is rich in fatty acids (FAs). To understand evolution of noni taste preference, we characterized behavioral and cellular responses to noni-associated FAs in three related drosophilids. We find that mixtures of sugar and noni FAs evoke strong aversion in the generalist species, but not in D. sechellia. Surveys of taste sensory responses reveal noni FA- and species-specific differences in at least two mechanisms – bitter neuron activation and sweet neuron inhibition – that correlate with shifts in noni preference. Chemoreceptor mutant analysis in D. melanogaster predicts that multiple genetic changes account for evolution of gustatory preference in D.sechellia.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-16T06:02:52.748Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/srrd35bxcm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/srrd35bxcm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Manali","last_name":"Dey","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Behavioral Neuroscience","Drosophila","Gustatory System"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":128574,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":161,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":128574,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128074,"restricted":63,"embargoed":437},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2161},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1331},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":592},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":457},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":429},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":406},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":298},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":256}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":125975},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1896},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":59},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":38},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":866},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":762},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":493},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}